  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nStrong surface high pressure is centered nearly over Ohio, still \npromoting very light winds over central IL with weak pressure \ngradients across this area. Mixed layer dewpoints were running \naround the upper 20s to around 30 yesterday afternoon, suggesting \nthat tonight's lows, expected to reach the upper 20s could \nresult in some patchy, locally dense ground fog by morning. \nModel guidance isn't inconsistent with this scenario with \nseveral models producing isolated spots of lowered visibility, \nhowever dewpoints in surface obs have been following a subtle \ndownward trend without much reduction in visibility, suggesting \nlow level moisture has been frosting out rather than forming \nfog. Still think localized fog is possible, but it is unclear if\nit will be prevalent enough for adding to gridded forecast at \nthis time.\n\nHigh pressure will gradually shift eastward toward the mid Atlantic \ncoast the next few days, with southerly flow increasing gradually \nday by day, as well as temperatures. Expect mid 50s highs today, \nupper 50s to around 60 Tuesday, and mainly lower 60s Wednesday. Lows \nshould reach lower to mid 30s tonight, mid to upper 30s Tuesday \nnight, and lower to mid 40s Wednesday night. Winds should reach a \nbreezy 10-15 mph with gusts 15-25 mph by Wednesday afternoon.\n\nA low moving from the upper Midwest into Ontario Thursday has \ntrended to a more northern track, which will leave a cold front \nstalling out before reaching central IL, and precipitation \nessentially ruled out for central IL. The warming trend will \nthus continue for a few more days, with highs in the mid 60s \nThursday and mid to upper 60s Friday. \n\nModels have come into better agreement for a weekend weather system, \nleaning toward a surface low tracking through the upper Midwest, and \na faster open-wave upper low, which looks to drag a cold front \nthrough central IL late Saturday. This yields 20-50 percent chances \nfor measurable precipitation west of I-57 by Saturday evening, and \n30-40 percent chances across the area Saturday night. Ensembles \ndepict low chances (10-30%) for a combined 100+ J/kg CAPE and 30+ kt \ndeep layer shear with this system for central IL. Will need to keep \nan eye on the development of a sufficient corridor of buoyancy for \npotential inclusion of thunderstorms, but at this time chances \nappear too low this far northeast. Light amounts of precipitation \nare likely for most areas, with model ensembles indicating only a 20-\n30 percent chance of exceeding a quarter inch, and thus little \nchance of seeing any meaningful drought relief.\n\nDry conditions should arrive by Sunday behind Saturday's cold front, \nwith temperatures trending downward, and highs mainly lower to mid \n50s.",
